    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | KS note: 'Tenant waives all liability of Landlord' language is exculpation-flavored, same category of soft spot as tenants-property-insurance. Extended to KS applying the same risk-tolerance precedent Taylor set for that clause (K.S.A. 58-2547(b): unenforceable-only unless knowing use of a known-prohibited term is shown) — not re-flagged as a fresh open item, but documented here in case Taylor wants to revisit the whole category at once. | NE extended (session 2, whole-library generic-clause audit): generic mechanics, no NE-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with NE findings from this project. | States KS removed (session 5, full liability-audit fix): superseded by state-specific override(s) addressing K.S.A. 58-2547(a)(4)/(a)(1) and/or Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1415(1)(d) exposure found in this session's full-library liability audit. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): generic mechanics compatible; note MN's habitability covenant (§ 504B.161, subd. 1(5)) requires heat at a minimum of 68°F Oct 1–Apr 30, which this clause's 'as otherwise required by applicable law' language already covers. | CORRECTION (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): MN tag added earlier this session was premature and has been REMOVED. This clause contains an exculpatory liability-waiver sentence ('Tenant waives all liability of Landlord for any interruption...') matching the pattern KS and NE found PROHIBITED under their general prohibited-lease-provisions statutes (§ 58-2547, § 76-1415), and which WY only confirmed safe after directly reading all 11 sections of its own Article 12. Minnesota's statute walk this session did NOT find a single consolidated 'prohibited lease provisions' statute like CO/KS/NE — instead MN has scattered per-section non-waiver rules — but that is not the same as a confirmed absence of a general exculpatory-clause prohibition, which may exist elsewhere in MN law (consumer protection statutes, case law) outside Chapter 504B. Leaving MN untagged until checked directly, consistent with WY's methodology rather than assuming safety from silence. | NE RE-AUDIT 2026-08-31 — REWORDED, and the diagnosis differs from the one flagged going in. §76-1415(1)(d) read from primary source (nebraskalegislature.gov): it bars exculpation or limitation of landlord liability 'arising due to ACTIVE AND ACTIONABLE NEGLIGENCE of the landlord.' This clause is already self-limited to 'causes beyond Landlord's reasonable control,' and a cause beyond the landlord's reasonable control is by definition not the landlord's active and actionable negligence — so the ORIGINAL NE SESSION'S REASONING ON (d) WAS CORRECT and no gross-negligence carve-out is needed here. That is why this clause differs from tenants-property-insurance-ne, parking-ne, and storage-space-ne: those three carried BLANKET disclaimers with no limiting qualifier, so they genuinely needed the carve-out. The apparent 'fixed four of five' asymmetry is not an oversight; the fifth clause needed a different and smaller fix. THE REAL EXPOSURE IS §76-1415(1)(a), NOT (d): the phrase 'TENANT WAIVES all liability of Landlord' is a tenant-side waiver, and (a) bars a rental agreement providing that the tenant 'agrees to waive or to forego rights or remedies under the Uniform Residential Landlord and Tenant Act.' Even where the practical scope is narrow, waiver-framed language invites the (a) argument for free. Recast as an allocation ('Landlord is not liable') rather than a tenant waiver. Substance unchanged; exposure removed. §5a.1 PROPAGATION JUDGMENT — UNIFORM, not state-driven. Applied to all three tagged states (CO;WY;NE). CO: §38-12-801(3) voids waivers of quiet enjoyment and of good faith and fair dealing, so waiver-framed phrasing is mildly worse there too — the reword is beneficial. WY: no prohibited-provisions statute at all (confirmed WY re-audit), so the reword is neutral-to-harmless. No state is disadvantaged, no state needs an override, and a NE-only override was deliberately NOT created because the improvement is not Nebraska-specific. Notes owed in the CO and WY logs. | ND ADDED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit, exhaustive generic-clause audit prompted by the checklist-to-CSV reconciliation screen). ND's ORIGINAL whole-library audit was written up in its log but largely never executed against the CSV -- ND was shipped 'complete' with 18 displayable LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 61-75 for CO/WY/KS/NE/MN. This row is generic boilerplate carrying no ND-specific statutory interaction; ND's ch. 47-16 was read in full from primary text this session and no conflict was identified. LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK: North Dakota has NO enumerated prohibited-lease-provisions statute (confirmed this session -- § 47-16-02 is a maximum-term cap, and § 47-16-13.3 supplies only case-by-case unconscionability). The exculpation exposure that forced fixes to this clause family in KS and NE therefore does not arise for ND, same reasoning as the WY addendum. 5a.1 JUDGMENT: UNIFORM -- states field changed, clause TEXT unchanged, no re-review owed on existing tags. | SD ADDED 2026-09-07 (SD re-audit, Tier A of the L.2 exhaustive generic-clause audit). SD displayed only 13 LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 60-75 for every other state; 46 generics had no SD tag and no SD override. Triaged per L.2, not bulk-extended. Tier A = boilerplate with no SD statutory interaction, or self-limiting text that defers to applicable law. S5a.1 JUDGMENT: adding a state tag changes `states` only - no bodyText, rule_type or content_type change - so no other tagged state's rendering is affected and NO propagation to other state logs is owed. State-driven, not uniform.",
