  "default-by-tenant": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-20",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-06",
    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself EDITED this session | CORRECTED 2026-08-20 (caught by Claude CLI review): added explicit carve-out that an unpaid late fee alone cannot trigger termination/eviction, consistent with CO law that a late fee is distinct from rent and cannot be grounds for eviction on its own. WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | MN extended (Minnesota session, CO;WY-scoping resolution): cure-period language defers to 'applicable law' rather than hardcoding a number, so it doesn't shorten any MN statutory cure right (MN has no general statutory cure period for lease violations, confirmed absent this session). Fee-shifting sentence is already mutual ('prevailing party') — compatible with MN's automatic-mutuality rule (§504B.172), no conflict. | Cross-state plain-language cleanup (Minnesota session, follow-up to MN's plain-language audit): long compound sentence split into shorter ones, no substantive change. Not a legal requirement for this clause's other tagged states (only MN's Plain Language Contract Act, Minn. Stat. §325G.31, was confirmed to apply this project) — done as a general readability improvement since the pattern was found library-wide, not MN-specific. | ND extended (North Dakota session 1, CO;WY-maintenance-scoping review resolved by reading the full CO and MN decision logs directly): defers to 'applicable law' for cure timing, no ND-specific conflict. Fee-shifting language ('prevailing party') matches N.D.C.C. § 47-16-13.6 exactly, which awards attorney's fees 'to the prevailing party' -- mutual, not one-way. This resolves ND's piece of the standing fee-shifting-mutuality question (though the broader cross-state consolidation across CO/WY/KS/NE/MN remains a separate open item). | 5a.1 RETROACTIVE CLASSIFICATION 2026-08-28: the prevailing-party attorney-fee rewrite here was STATE-DRIVEN (forced by C.R.S. 38-12-801(3)(a)(II)), not uniform, and was inherited by WY/MN/ND/SD without classification - the exact failure mode 5a.1 exists to prevent. Checked for WY 2026-08-28: WY has NO reciprocity statute (see edu-no-fee-reciprocity-wy), so a landlord-only clause would be enforceable there and the mutual language is a voluntary giveaway. Taylor's decision 2026-08-28: KEEP mutual language for WY. MN, ND, and SD remain unclassified for this edit. | CROSS-STATE FEE-SHIFTING CONSOLIDATION COMPLETED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit). The standing open item -- 'is this clause's fee language genuinely mutual, and does it work in every tagged state?' -- is resolved. Operative sentence: 'To the extent permitted under applicable law, the prevailing party may recover from the other party court costs and reasonable attorneys' fees and expenses incurred in connection with any legal proceedings related to this Lease.' FIVE DISTINCT STATE ARCHITECTURES, not one question: (1) CO -- a landlord-only fee clause is VOID and the lease must be drafted mutual; 'prevailing party' SATISFIES this. (2) MN -- fee clauses are made mutual BY OPERATION OF LAW regardless of lease text; consistent, no conflict. (3) KS (K.S.A. 58-2547) and NE (§ 76-1415(1)(c)) -- attorney-fee clauses are BANNED OUTRIGHT in either direction; this clause is correctly NOT tagged to either, and a library-wide check confirmed ZERO LEASE_CLAUSE rows tagged KS or NE contain attorney-fee language. (4) ND -- § 47-16-13.6 awards fees to 'the prevailing party' by statute; clause language matches exactly. (5) WY -- American Rule, no reciprocity statute, contract governs. OPEN FLAG FOR WYOMING, surfaced not resolved: per edu-no-fee-reciprocity-wy the Wyoming Supreme Court requires a contract to 'unequivocally provide' for fee recovery before fees may be awarded. This clause hedges twice -- 'TO THE EXTENT PERMITTED UNDER APPLICABLE LAW' makes the entitlement conditional on an external referent, and 'MAY recover' is permissive rather than mandatory. Under a strict unequivocal-provision standard that phrasing may not suffice, which would leave a WY landlord with NO fee recovery in a state whose default is that each side bears its own. No Wyoming case applying the standard to comparable hedged language was located, and no edit is made on that basis -- flagged for Taylor. Note the hedge exists for a reason: it is what makes the clause safe to share with CO and MN. Any WY fix should be a WY-specific override, NOT an edit to the shared text. | WYOMING FLAG RETRACTED 2026-09-06 after reading the actual case law rather than this library's characterisation of it. NO WY-SPECIFIC OVERRIDE IS NEEDED. The Wyoming standard is EXPRESS CONTRACTUAL AUTHORISATION, not a heightened magic-words test: Cowardin v. Finnerty (Wyo. 1999) -- 'absent statutory or contract language expressly authorizing recovery of attorney fees, parties are responsible for payment of their own attorney fees'; Emken, 2006 WY 112, ¶ 8 -- 'a prevailing party may be reimbursed for its attorney fees when express statutory or contractual authorization exists'; Thorkildsen v. Belden, 2012 WY 8; Circle Resources v. Hassler, 2023 WY 22, ¶ 8 -- 'A prevailing party may, however, be reimbursed for attorneys' fees when provided for by contract or statute.' The Wyoming failure cases are ones where the contract said NOTHING about fees. Cowardin is explicit: 'Given the absence of any reference to attorney fees in the purchase agreement, Sellers were not entitled to recover attorney fees.' THIS CLAUSE EXPRESSLY REFERS TO ATTORNEY FEES, NAMES THE PREVAILING PARTY, AND USES THE COURT'S OWN FORMULATION. The supposed defect -- 'may recover' being permissive -- is the verb the Wyoming Supreme Court itself uses in stating the rule. 'To the extent permitted under applicable law' is a savings clause; no Wyoming authority was located suggesting a savings clause defeats otherwise-express authorisation, and the circularity concern raised earlier in this session was speculative. PROCESS NOTE: the flag originated from this library's own edu-no-fee-reciprocity-wy row, which characterises the standard as requiring a contract to 'unequivocally provide' for fees. The cases say 'expressly authorizing' / 'express... authorization'. Raising a structural change to a four-state shared clause on a secondary characterisation, without reading the cases, was the Addendum L.5 failure committed in the same session that wrote L.5.",
  },
