  "holdover": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-21",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-03",
    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE added (session 1): Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1437(3) allows up to 3x periodic rent or 3x actual damages for willful bad-faith holdover — higher than this clause's double-rent figure, so the clause still holds up in NE without an override (per Taylor's read, Flag 6). See edu-holdover-ne for the full NE figure and a note to revisit the double-rent baseline once more states are surveyed. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): generic mechanics, already vetted against KS/NE's prohibited-lease-provisions statutes (the strictest bar found in this project to date) since this clause was already tagged KS;NE; no MN-specific statutory language or number identified in this session's statute walk that conflicts. No exculpatory/liability-waiver language present. | Cross-state plain-language cleanup (Minnesota session, follow-up to MN's plain-language audit): long compound sentence split into shorter ones, no substantive change. Not a legal requirement for this clause's other tagged states (only MN's Plain Language Contract Act, Minn. Stat. §325G.31, was confirmed to apply this project) — done as a general readability improvement since the pattern was found library-wide, not MN-specific. | SHARED-CLAUSE PASS 2026-09-03 (§5a.1). EDIT: removed the hardcoded 'double the Monthly Rent ... or the maximum amount allowed under applicable law, if less' and replaced it with a pure deferral to the statutory maximum. §5a.1 JUDGMENT: **STATE-DRIVEN** — the holdover figure genuinely differs per state — but the EDIT itself is uniform-safe and no per-state override is required, because each state's figure is already carried by its own education row. WHY THE OLD TEXT WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is why leaving it was not an option: (1) OVER-PROMISED in MN — Minnesota has NO holdover multiplier at all (§504B.141 and the eviction chapter, confirmed at MN re-audit), so 'double the Monthly Rent' described a remedy that does not exist; the self-limiting tail made it lawful but misleading. (2) UNDER-CLAIMED in NE — Nebraska allows three months' periodic rent or threefold actual damages, whichever is greater, PLUS statutory attorney fees (§76-1437(3)), all of which EXCEED double monthly rent. The old 'or the maximum allowed ... if less' language could only ever cap DOWNWARD, so a Nebraska landlord relying on the clause would recover materially less than the statute allows. A ceiling-only formulation silently forfeits recovery in any state whose statutory figure is higher than the hardcoded one. KS is unaffected either way (1.5x cap under K.S.A. 58-2570(c), already below double, per edu-holdover-ks). GAP SURFACED, NOT CLOSED: CO and WY have NO holdover-damages education row and their statutory figures have never been verified in this project. The 'double' figure appears to have originated as Colorado's, but that has not been confirmed against C.R.S. Recommend verifying CO and WY holdover figures and adding edu-holdover-co / edu-holdover-wy, matching KS/NE/MN. | FOLLOW-UP 2026-09-03: the gap flagged in the shared-clause pass is now closed, and the answer is worse than expected. THE 'DOUBLE THE MONTHLY RENT' FIGURE HAD NO STATUTORY BASIS IN ANY OF THE FIVE TAGGED STATES. CO: no multiplier, damages = reasonable rental value (§13-40-123, Behr v. Burge). WY: no multiplier, actual damages plus 10% interest (§1-21-1211(b), provisional). KS: 1.5x cap, BELOW double (K.S.A. 58-2570(c)). NE: three months' periodic rent or threefold actual plus attorney fees, ABOVE double (§76-1437(3)). MN: no multiplier at all. So the number was wrong in every direction simultaneously — too high in three states, too low in one, and baseless in the two where it was assumed to have originated. The shared-clause pass hypothesized it 'appears to have originated as Colorado's'; that hypothesis is now DISPROVED. It appears to be generic template boilerplate that entered the library before any state research and was never questioned, and it survived four state re-audits. Removing it was correct. Recording the provenance because it is the clearest example in the project of an unsourced number propagating through a shared clause: nothing in the library ever asserted where 'double' came from, and every state's own research quietly contradicted it.",
  },
