    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE extended (session 2, whole-library generic-clause audit): generic mechanics, no NE-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with NE findings from this project. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): generic mechanics, already vetted against KS/NE's prohibited-lease-provisions statutes (the strictest bar found in this project to date) since this clause was already tagged KS;NE; no MN-specific statutory language or number identified in this session's statute walk that conflicts. No exculpatory/liability-waiver language present. | SHARED-CLAUSE PASS 2026-09-03 (§5a.1). EDIT: added a saving clause deferring to any statutory method, form, timing, or content requirement, and disclaiming any implied designation of an alternative delivery method. §5a.1 JUDGMENT: **UNIFORM** — every tagged state specifies delivery methods per-statute, so deferring is correct in all of them; safe to inherit, no per-state override needed. TRIGGER: the MN re-audit found Minnesota specifies delivery section-by-section with conflicting requirements (§504B.321 subd. 1a(b) personal or first class mail ONLY, and failure means dismissal + expungement; §504B.271 personal service OR first class AND certified PLUS posting; §504B.365 subd. 3(g) mail PLUS a telephone attempt; §504B.206 subd. 1(c) the only electronic-permissive one). The prior text named only WHERE notices go and was silent on HOW, which risked being read as permitting any delivery to a valid address. Note the original RA-24 concern was partly overstated: this clause never actually designated email or a portal — it was silent, not wrong. The edit closes the silence rather than correcting an assertion. | ND ADDED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit, exhaustive generic-clause audit prompted by the checklist-to-CSV reconciliation screen). ND's ORIGINAL whole-library audit was written up in its log but largely never executed against the CSV -- ND was shipped 'complete' with 18 displayable LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 61-75 for CO/WY/KS/NE/MN. This row is generic boilerplate carrying no ND-specific statutory interaction; ND's ch. 47-16 was read in full from primary text this session and no conflict was identified. LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK: North Dakota has NO enumerated prohibited-lease-provisions statute (confirmed this session -- § 47-16-02 is a maximum-term cap, and § 47-16-13.3 supplies only case-by-case unconscionability). The exculpation exposure that forced fixes to this clause family in KS and NE therefore does not arise for ND, same reasoning as the WY addendum. 5a.1 JUDGMENT: UNIFORM -- states field changed, clause TEXT unchanged, no re-review owed on existing tags. | SD ADDED 2026-09-07 (SD re-audit, Tier A of the L.2 exhaustive generic-clause audit). SD displayed only 13 LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 60-75 for every other state; 46 generics had no SD tag and no SD override. Triaged per L.2, not bulk-extended. Tier A = boilerplate with no SD statutory interaction, or self-limiting text that defers to applicable law. S5a.1 JUDGMENT: adding a state tag changes `states` only - no bodyText, rule_type or content_type change - so no other tagged state's rendering is affected and NO propagation to other state logs is owed. State-driven, not uniform.",
