  "smoke-detector-duty-ne": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-23",
    lastChecked: "2026-08-31",
    notes: "Neb. Rev. Stat. §81-5,144 — pass-2 canvass catch (session 2), missed entirely in pass 1. Sits outside the core landlord-tenant title (Chapter 81, State Fire Marshal statutes), the same pattern that made Kansas's service-animal-fraud statute (Chapter 39) easy to miss on a first pass — worth remembering for future states that a state's core landlord-tenant chapter doesn't capture everything relevant. No CO/WY/KS analog logged in this project to date; add as a new canvass row for future states. | NE RE-AUDIT 2026-08-31 — CITATION CONFIRMED, CLAUSE MATERIALLY INCOMPLETE. §81-5,144 read from nebraskalegislature.gov and Justia; it exists and says what the row claimed, so a citation-existence screen would have passed it. Reading the full text surfaced FOUR omissions in the clause built on it: (1) LANDLORD DUTY OMITTED — 'The owner... shall provide a notice to such occupant containing instructions for the testing of the device.' An affirmative landlord obligation that was simply missing. (2) BATTERY ALLOCATION OMITTED — the statute expressly provides that 'deficiencies shall not include a worn battery or other replaceable energy unit,' that the occupant is responsible for replacement, and that the battery must be operating when the occupant takes possession. This is a useful, specific allocation the clause gave up for no reason. (3) TENANT-DUTY TRIGGER OMITTED — the tenant testing duty attaches only where the unit 'is being occupied for one month or more by the same occupant,' not to every tenancy. (4) LANDLORD CORRECTION DUTY OMITTED — the owner 'shall correct any reported deficiencies.' All four now in the clause. SCOPE CAVEAT, flagged not resolved: the underlying requirement to HAVE detectors lives in the companion section §81-5,142 and is date-triggered — dwellings, apartment houses, lodging-house guest rooms and dormitories 'constructed or remodeled on or after January 1, 1982.' §81-5,144 allocates responsibility for detectors; §81-5,142 creates the mandate. The row previously cited only §81-5,144 and stated an unconditional duty. Whether a pre-1982, never-remodeled building falls outside the mandate entirely is a real question this session did not resolve — §81-5,142's own text has not been read, only NCSL's summary of it. Practically low-stakes (installing detectors is cheap and universally advisable) but the row should not imply a statutory mandate that may not reach every building. See edu-smoke-detector-scope-ne.",
  },
  "tenants-property-insurance-ne": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONSTRAINED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-23",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1415(1)(d). Resolves edu-negligence-carveout-flag-ne (session 4): the generic clause's blanket 'Landlord is not liable' sentence is narrowed to exclude only ordinary negligence, explicitly preserving liability for Landlord's gross negligence or willful misconduct — tracks Nebraska's negligence-specific exculpation ban precisely, and mirrors the carve-out a real NE commercial lease product (ILRG/PublicLegal) uses for the same clause. Taylor's decision (per chat): draft the NE-specific override rather than leave the blanket generic in place. The parallel KS question (flagged in the KS log's own notes on this clause, broader exculpation ban) remains separately open — not resolved here, since KS's ban is broader than a negligence-only trigger and may need different treatment. | CORRECTION 2026-09-07 (SD re-audit session, cross-state follow-up): the carve-out read 'except to the extent caused by Landlord's GROSS negligence or willful misconduct'. **Gross negligence is a higher bar than the statute's.** Neb. Rev. Stat. S76-1415(1)(d) voids a provision whereby the tenant agrees to the exculpation or limitation of any liability of the landlord 'arising due to ACTIVE AND ACTIONABLE NEGLIGENCE of the landlord'. Active-but-ordinary negligence - a landlord who leaves a gate open, or ignores a known broken lock - is actively and actionably negligent without being grossly negligent, so the prior wording continued to purport to exculpate precisely the band S76-1415(1)(d) voids, and was void pro tanto in that band. Carve-out now tracks the statute's own phrase. Willful misconduct retained: it sits above the statutory floor and is separately unexculpable. **Deliberately NOT widened to 'any negligence'** - NE voids only ACTIVE and actionable negligence, so a blanket negligence carve-out would surrender passive negligence Nebraska permits. S5a.1: NE-only override; the generic (CO;WY;ND;SD) is untouched and no propagation is owed - and the generic's blanket sentence remains correct for those four, where ordinary-negligence exculpation is enforceable (SD S53-9-3 per Holzer/Domson; ND N.D.C.C. 9-08-02; WY S1-21-1202(d); CO S38-12-801 bans specific waivers only).",
  },
