    lastChecked: "2026-09-03",
    notes: "MN added (Minnesota session, CO;WY-scoping resolution, supersedes generic landlord-maintenance): Minn. Stat. § 504B.161. Follows the established WY/KS/NE pattern (habitability-baseline-wy/ks/ne) of building a dedicated clause once a state's habitability covenant carries specifics the generic doesn't capture. MN's specifics: the 68°F Oct 1–Apr 30 heat minimum (already reflected in services-utilities-provided's note but not previously in a maintenance-duty clause), an explicit extermination duty, and a cost-effectiveness-gated energy-efficiency duty — none of which the generic landlord-maintenance clause states. Non-waivable except for a narrow tenant-delegation option (§504B.161, subd. 2) requiring adequate consideration and a conspicuous writing, which cannot waive common-area maintenance — not built into this clause as an option this session, flagged for a future session if Taylor wants to offer it. | MN re-audit 2026-09-03: confirmed Minn. Stat. §504B.161 subd. 1(b) makes these covenants NON-WAIVABLE — 'The parties ... may not waive or modify the covenants imposed by this section.' Subd. 2 allows a narrow exception: the landlord and tenant may agree in writing that the TENANT will perform specified repairs or maintenance, but only if supported by adequate consideration AND set forth in a conspicuous writing, and such an agreement may not waive subd. 1 or relieve the landlord of the duty to maintain COMMON AREAS. The original MN session did not record this. No body-text change made — the clause already states the duties affirmatively and does not attempt a waiver — but FLAGGED FOR TAYLOR: if Steinoak ever offers a tenant-performs-maintenance option for MN, it must carry adequate consideration, conspicuous formatting, and a common-areas carve-out, or it is void. | MN re-audit 2026-09-03, canvass pass 2: 'habitability operational duties' canvass topic resolved for MN — PRESENT and covered by this clause plus edu-renters-insurance-limits-mn. Enforcement mechanics confirmed as living outside this clause by design: rent escrow (§504B.385), tenant remedies action (§504B.395), emergency relief (§504B.381), and the code-violation notice track (§504B.185). Those are tenant-side procedures, not lease terms, consistent with the project's treatment of remedies elsewhere.",
