  "smoke-detector-duty-nd": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-24",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-06",
    notes: "N.D.C.C. § 23-13-15, WHOLE SECTION read from primary text at ND re-audit 2026-09-06 (ndlegis.gov/cencode/t23c13.pdf). Previously FindLaw-sourced and never primary-verified. Title 23 is Health and Safety -- outside the core landlord-tenant title. CORRECTED, three defects: (a) The prior body opened 'Landlord will install and maintain a smoke detection system', then said the tenant maintains in a single-family rental -- internally contradictory. The statute assigns maintenance and inspection in a single-family rental dwelling to the TENANT; the landlord's install-and-ensure-operation duty attaches to 'other dwellings'. Rewritten to track the statute's own split. (b) The prior row OMITTED § 23-13-15(1)'s opening exception -- 'All residential rental property with the exception of property covered by section 23-09-02.1'. § 23-09-02.1 sits in ch. 23-09 (Food and Lodging Establishments and Assisted Living Facilities) and requires LODGING ESTABLISHMENTS and ASSISTED LIVING FACILITIES to install devices approved by the department in cooperation with the state fire marshal instead. Those properties are outside § 23-13-15 entirely. DO NOT CONFLATE with § 47-16-19's 'lodgings' -- that is a landlord-tenant term for room/periodic rentals; 'lodging establishment' in ch. 23-09 is a licensed hospitality category. Different concepts, similar words. (c) The prior row folded the deaf-tenant exemption into the clause body ('unless Landlord's total rental property is a single building of four units or fewer'). Removed from tenant-facing text: § 23-13-15(2)'s exemption is a fact about the landlord's whole portfolio, not a term of this tenancy, and reciting it in the lease tells a deaf tenant their request may be refused on facts they cannot verify. Exemption preserved here: a landlord is not subject to (2) if their rental property does not exceed one building AND that building does not exceed four residential dwelling units. Both limbs required. ALSO NOW RECORDED: § 23-13-15(3) preserves ch. 54-21.3 (State Building Code) for NEWLY CONSTRUCTED residences -- this section does not displace it. § 23-13-15(4): willful failure to INSTALL (not to maintain) is a class B misdemeanour. FLAG CORRECTED 2026-09-06 after reading N.D. Admin. Code ch. 33-33-05 from primary text (ndlegis.gov/prod/acdata/pdf/33-33-05.pdf). An earlier note in this same session identified ch. 33-33-05 (Smoke Detector Rules) as the fire marshal rules incorporated by reference into § 23-13-15. THAT WAS WRONG on two counts. (1) Every section of ch. 33-33-05 carries 'General Authority: NDCC 23-01-03(3), 23-09-02.1' and 'Law Implemented: NDCC 23-09-02.1' -- it implements § 23-09-02.1, which is the section § 23-13-15 EXCLUDES. Ch. 33-33-05 governs lodging establishments and assisted living facilities, i.e. exactly the properties outside this clause. (2) They are State Department of Health rules (Title 33), not fire marshal rules. INCORPORATION-BY-REFERENCE NOW RESOLVED 2026-09-06: N.D. Admin. Code art. 45-18, ch. 45-18-01, read from primary (ndlegis.gov/information/acdata/pdf/45-18-01.pdf), effective 2024-01-01. § 45-18-01-04 defines the State Fire Code as (1) the fire-safe construction and operation provisions of the State Building Code effective 2023-01-01 per N.D.C.C. § 54-21.3-03, and (2) the INTERNATIONAL FIRE CODE, 2021 edition (International Code Council), with enumerated ND modifications. So the compliance standard in this clause is a PRIVATELY PUBLISHED MODEL CODE, obtainable per § 45-18-01-05 only from the ICC or NFPA. See edu-fire-code-standard-nd. Recording the mechanism of the error because it is instructive: R.23 of this session flagged the risk of confusing § 47-16-19 'lodgings' with ch. 23-09 'lodging establishment', and the very next flag written in that same row fell into an adjacent version of the same trap -- attributing a lodging-establishment rule to the general residential-rental statute.",
  },
