  "fire-casualty-termination-mn": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-03",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-03",
    notes: "MN added (MN re-audit 2026-09-03, canvass pass 2) — resolves the 'fire/casualty damage — tenant termination or rent-reduction right' canvass topic for MN. Minn. Stat. §504B.131, read in full (it is a single short paragraph). KS has fire-casualty-termination-ks; MN had no row. IMPORTANT ARCHITECTURAL DIFFERENCE, resolving the uncertainty MN §13 flagged and never closed: Minnesota provides ONLY a vacate-and-surrender right. There is NO proportional rent-reduction / rent-abatement mechanic for PARTIAL damage of the kind KS and NE have. A Minnesota tenant whose unit is partly damaged but still habitable does not get an abatement under this section — their remedy runs through the habitability covenant (§504B.161) and the tenant-remedies actions (§§504B.381-.471) instead. Do not inherit the KS/NE framing. UNUSUAL FEATURE: the section is DEFAULT-BUT-WAIVABLE — 'A tenant or occupant may expressly agree otherwise except as prohibited by section 504B.161.' So unlike most of Chapter 504B, this right CAN be contracted around, but only down to the non-waivable habitability floor. This also answers the checklist's 'environmental-event lease termination as an opt-in landlord right' topic for MN: it is the inverse — a default tenant right that may be expressly waived, not an opt-in landlord right. FLAGGED FOR TAYLOR: because the right is waivable, Steinoak could offer a variant clause that contracts around it; not drafted, since doing so trades a tenant protection for landlord benefit and is a product-policy call, not a legal one.",
  },
