  "landlords-access-sd": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-07",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "SD re-audit 2026-09-07 (Tier B resolved). Supersedes generic `landlords-access`, withheld from SD because it carries a flat 24-hour period and none of SDCL 43-32-32's mandatory notice CONTENT: 'The notice shall specify date or dates of entry, a period of time during normal business hours for entry, and the purpose of intended entry. The notice shall also specify a means for which the tenant may request to reschedule the entry.' (SL 2014 ch 191 S4.) Four mandatory elements, all 'shall'; the generic supplies none, so tagging it would have shipped an SD entry clause omitting every element SD requires. Same override pattern ND uses (landlords-access-nd), drafted from SD's own statute rather than adapted from ND's per the Dakota-ancestry rule. NUANCE PRESERVED: SD's 24 hours is a PRESUMPTION of reasonableness, not a floor, and 43-32-32 allows 'alternate methods of notification or times for entry... mutually agreed upon between the landlord and tenant in the lease' - hence 'presumed reasonable' rather than 'at least'. Also unblocks `inspection-rights`, which defers to the lease's Access & Entry terms and pointed at nothing for SD until now. [Source-integrity: full ch.43-32 text via consumer.sd.gov/docs/LLTen_Statutes43-32.pdf, CONFIRMED STALE as to 43-32-24 (prints 'two weeks', source line omits SL 2026 ch 179). All other sections used carry source lines ending 1939-2022 and were unamended 2024-2026. See Addendum L.8.]",
  },
  "holdover-sd": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-07",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "SD re-audit 2026-09-07 (Tier B resolved). Supersedes generic `holdover`, withheld from SD because it promises 'holdover damages in the maximum amount permitted by applicable law for each day Tenant remains in possession'. SD HAS NO HOLDOVER DAMAGES MULTIPLIER - no double rent, no per-diem penalty (see edu-holdover-mechanics-sd). In SD that phrase resolves to zero, so the generic would promise the landlord a remedy that does not exist - the inverse of the usual defect: not a misstated rule but a clause whose operative term is NULL in this jurisdiction. Replaced with SDCL 43-32-14's actual mechanic: 'If a lessee of real property remains in possession thereof after the expiration of the hiring and the lessor accepts rent from him, the parties are presumed to have renewed the hiring on the same terms and for the same time, not exceeding one year.' The generic's 'month-to-month basis' fallback was also wrong for SD - 43-32-14 renews for the SAME TIME as the original term, not monthly. Final sentence carries 43-32-15's scaling so the renewed term is terminable. ND is excluded from the generic and has no override; SD gets one rather than an exclusion because leaving SD clause-less would reproduce the omission failure mode this session exists to correct. [Source-integrity: full ch.43-32 text via consumer.sd.gov/docs/LLTen_Statutes43-32.pdf, CONFIRMED STALE as to 43-32-24 (prints 'two weeks', source line omits SL 2026 ch 179). All other sections used carry source lines ending 1939-2022 and were unamended 2024-2026. See Addendum L.8.]",
  },
  "landlord-maintenance-sd": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-07",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "SD re-audit 2026-09-07 (Tier B resolved). Supersedes generic `landlord-maintenance` (tagged CO only), which opens 'Subject to Tenant's own maintenance obligations under this Lease, Landlord will maintain...'. That subordination is the problem in SD: SDCL 43-32-8 allocates the repair duty to the lessor and states expressly 'The parties to a lease or hiring of residential premises may not waive or modify the requirements imposed by this section'. A clause making the lessor's duty subject to the tenant's obligations is arguably a modification and void pro tanto. Drafted to track 43-32-8's own language and to state the non-waiver and the repairs-in-lieu-of-rent exception on the face of the clause. Closing sentence preserves the 43-32-9 remedies (repair-and-deduct, vacate-and-discharge, rent escrow where repair cost exceeds one month's rent) without restating them. CROSS-STATE BACKLOG: the CO;WY-only scoping of the generic maintenance pair has been open since the MN session; this resolves SD's instance only, by override rather than extension. Why the generic is CO-only remains open for CO/WY. [Source-integrity: full ch.43-32 text via consumer.sd.gov/docs/LLTen_Statutes43-32.pdf, CONFIRMED STALE as to 43-32-24 (prints 'two weeks', source line omits SL 2026 ch 179). All other sections used carry source lines ending 1939-2022 and were unamended 2024-2026. See Addendum L.8.]",
  },
  "pet-policy-sd": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-07",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-07",
    notes: "SD re-audit 2026-09-07. Supersedes the generic `pet-policy` (CO;WY;ND), which was the last Tier B item held back from the L.2 generic-clause audit. Option C of three presented to Taylor and approved by him. TWO CHANGES from the generic, both narrow: (1) The phrase 'without liability to Tenant' is STRUCK. SDCL 53-9-3 voids any contract term whose object is to exempt a party from responsibility for 'violation of law whether willful or negligent'. A blanket no-liability exemption attached to an entry-and-remove right reaches the tail cases where the entry is not clearly lawful - tenant incapacitated but the animal in no distress, or a good-faith belief that turns out wrong - and in SD an unlawful entry is expensive: SDCL 43-32-6 gives the tenant two months' rent plus return of any advance rent and deposit. Striking the phrase costs the landlord nothing they actually had, because the exemption would not have been enforced that far anyway. (2) Entry is now tied to the lease's Access and Entry section, with the statutory emergency/impracticability exception stated on the face of the clause. This closes an internal inconsistency created earlier in the same session: `landlords-access-sd` was written to carry SDCL 43-32-32's four mandatory notice elements (date(s), a period during normal business hours, purpose, and a means to request rescheduling), while the generic pet-policy granted a separate entry right that mentioned none of them. Two entry rights in one lease on different terms is a drafting defect independent of any statute. ASSESSMENT CORRECTED IN THE OPEN: this row was originally flagged as a clear 53-9-3 third-prong violation. On re-reading, the enumerated entry grounds - a vicious animal, a severely ill animal - are mostly genuine emergencies, and 43-32-32 expressly excepts emergencies from the notice requirement, so the entry itself is usually lawful. The risk is narrower than first stated and is confined to the non-emergency tail. Recorded because the earlier framing overstated it and the overstatement is part of the record. DOWNSIDE OF THE ALTERNATIVE, for the record: SD has no enumerated prohibited-terms statute (edu-no-prohibited-terms-list-sd), so an overbroad exculpatory phrase is not unlawful in SD - it simply would not be enforced to that extent. Tagging the generic as-is was a defensible option; the override was chosen for the entry-consistency reason as much as the 53-9-3 one. CROSS-STATE CANDIDATE, not acted on: ND is tagged on the generic and has N.D.C.C. 9-08-02, the direct counterpart to 53-9-3, so the same 'without liability' phrase plausibly has the same limit in ND. Per L.4 and the Dakota-ancestry rule this is recorded as a candidate for ND's next pass, NOT an inherited conclusion, and no ND row was touched. CO and WY unexamined for this point. S5a.1: this is a new override row, not an edit to the shared generic - the generic's text and its CO;WY;ND tags are unchanged, so no other state's rendering is affected and no propagation is owed.",
  },
